s ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\nNow, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{1}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.\n\nHence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to \n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n|  |  | ${x}_{3}$ |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince the two linearly independent solutions are already mutually orthogonal, we only need to normalize them. Hence, we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-6}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-7}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-8}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-9}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"] [:db/cas 17592186408021 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{3}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables.\n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we only need to normalize them. This gives the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{3}{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables.\n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we only need to normalize them. This gives the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186408051 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186408052 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-14 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-14 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186408058 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186408063 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186408064 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-14 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 28\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-14 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 28\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186408070 :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186408070 :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3\\times 3$ matrix, which will require finding $9$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-14 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 28\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3\\times 3$ matrix, which will require finding $9$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-14 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 28\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186408162 :question/problem "The matrix $D$ and the vector $b$ are given below.\nDetermine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(D).$ \nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}$, where ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, ${x}_{3}$, and ${x}_{4}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{d}_{1}+{x}_{2}{d}_{2}+{x}_{3}{d}_{3}+{x}_{4}{d}_{4},$ and ${d}_{1}$, ${d}_{2}$, ${d}_{3}$, and ${d}_{4}$ are respective columns of $D.$\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 5 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The matrix $D$ and the vector $b$ are given below.\nDetermine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(D).$ \nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}$, where ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, ${x}_{3}$, and ${x}_{4}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{d}_{1}+{x}_{2}{d}_{2}+{x}_{3}{d}_{3}+{x}_{4}{d}_{4},$ and ${d}_{1}$, ${d}_{2}$, ${d}_{3}$, and ${d}_{4}$ are respective columns of $D.$\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186408162 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(D),$ we need to check if the equation $Dx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[D\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[D\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 6 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 5 & -1 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 5 & -1 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}-2{x}_{4}=1 \\\\ 0=6 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}=-8 \\\\ {x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}-{x}_{4}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the  second equation, we get a false statement $0=6.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(D).$" "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(D),$ we need to check if the equation $Dx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[D\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[D\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 6 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 5 & -1 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 5 & -1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}-2{x}_{4}=1 \\\\ 0=6 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}=-8 \\\\ {x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}-{x}_{4}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the  second equation, we get a false statement $0=6.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(D).$"] [:db/cas 17592186408163 :question/problem "The matrix $K$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(K).$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ where ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{k}_{1}+{x}_{2}{k}_{2},$ and ${k}_{1},$ ${k}_{2}$ are the respective columns of $K.$\n\n$$\nK=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The matrix $K$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(K).$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ where ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{k}_{1}+{x}_{2}{k}_{2},$ and ${k}_{1},$ ${k}_{2}$ are the respective columns of $K.$\n\n$$\nK=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186408163 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(K),$ we need to check if the equation $Kx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[K\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[K\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -8 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -8 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-4{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -8 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -18\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ {x}_{2}=5 \\\\ 0=-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get the false statement $0=-18.$ \n\nTherefore, the system is inconsistent, which implies that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(K).$" "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(K),$ we need to check if the equation $Kx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[K\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[K\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -8 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -8 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-4{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -8 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -18\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ {x}_{2}=5 \\\\ 0=-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get the false statement $0=-18.$ \n\nTherefore, the system is inconsistent, which implies that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(K).$"] [:db/cas 17592186408164 :question/problem "The matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are given below.\nDetermine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(A).$ \nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}$, where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3},$ and ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, and ${a}_{3}$ are the respective columns of $A.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 10 & -7 \\\\ 3 & -12 & 10\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -13\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are given below.\nDetermine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(A).$ \nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}$, where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3},$ and ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, and ${a}_{3}$ are the respective columns of $A.$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 10 & -7 \\\\ 3 & -12 & 10\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -13\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186408164 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ we need to check if the equation $Ax=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 10 & -7 & 11 \\\\ 3 & -12 & 10 & -13\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -12 & 10 & -13\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=-4 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=3 \\\\ {x}_{3}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-1.$ Substituting it into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}-(-1) & =3 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} & =2 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-4+4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =-4+4(1)-3(-1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $Ax=b$ is consistent, which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(A).$ \n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -13\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}=3\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+1\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}+(-1)\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=3+1+(-1)=3.$" "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ we need to check if the equation $Ax=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 10 & -7 & 11 \\\\ 3 & -12 & 10 & -13\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -12 & 10 & -13\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=-4 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=3 \\\\ {x}_{3}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-1.$ Substituting it into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}-(-1) & =3 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} & =2 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-4+4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =-4+4(1)-3(-1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $Ax=b$ is consistent, which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(A).$ \n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -13\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}=3\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+1\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}+(-1)\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=3+1+(-1)=3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186408165 :question/problem "Consider the matrix given by $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $b=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ k+5\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Col}(A).$" "Consider the matrix given by $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $b=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ k+5\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $\\text{Col}(A).$"] [:db/cas 17592186408165 :question/worked-solution "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ then the equation $Ax=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 7 & -2 & k+5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & k+40\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k+30\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=-5 \\\\ {x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 0=k+30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=k+30,$ which means that $k=-30.$" "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ then the equation $Ax=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 7 & -2 & k+5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & k+40\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k+30\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=-5 \\\\ {x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 0=k+30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=k+30,$ which means that $k=-30.$"] [:db/cas 17592186409623 :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" "B={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186409625 :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}" "B={\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186409626 :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}" "B={\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186409627 :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}" "B={\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186409628 :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}" "B={\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186409634 :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ ${a}_{3},$ and ${a}_{4}$ are given below. Determine whether $B={{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}.$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3},$ where ${a}_{4}={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{4}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -22\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The vectors ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ ${a}_{3},$ and ${a}_{4}$ are given below. Determine whether $B={{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}.$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3},$ where ${a}_{4}={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{4}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -22\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186409634 :question/worked-solution "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}+{x}_{3}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}={{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -22\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=-4 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=4 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=-22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 5 & -22\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 5 & -22\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns. So,  $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that ${x}_{1}=2,$ ${x}_{2}=0,$ and ${x}_{3}=-6,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n2\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+0\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}+(-6)\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}={{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -22\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=2+0+(-6)=-4.$" "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}+{x}_{3}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}={{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -22\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=-4 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=4 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=-22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 5 & -22\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 5 & -22\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns. So,  $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that ${x}_{1}=2,$ ${x}_{2}=0,$ and ${x}_{3}=-6,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n2\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+0\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}+(-6)\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}={{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -22\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=2+0+(-6)=-4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186409635 :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ by inspection." "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ by inspection."] [:db/cas 17592186409635 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{2}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=1\\cdot {a}_{1}+1\\cdot {a}_{3}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{2}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is spanned by just ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}},{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}}.\n$$" "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{2}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=1\\cdot {a}_{1}+1\\cdot {a}_{3}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{2}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is spanned by just ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}},{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186409636 :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are given below. Determine whether $B={{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ where ${a}_{3}={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The vectors ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are given below. Determine whether $B={{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ where ${a}_{3}={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186409636 :question/worked-solution "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}={{[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=14 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 14 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the RREF matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So,  $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=-5,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n2\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+(-5)\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}={{[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot (-5)=-10.$" "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}={{\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nT