aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. Adding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}={b}_{1}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{1}={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=2({c}_{1}+{c}_{2})-{b}_{2}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}={c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4{b}_{1}-2{b}_{2} \\\\  & =4({c}_{1}+{c}_{2})-2({c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =4{c}_{1}+4{c}_{2}-2{c}_{1}-4{c}_{2} \\\\  & =2{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186410059 :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}},$ where ${c}_{1}=5{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=-3{b}_{1}?$" "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}},$ where ${c}_{1}=5{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=-3{b}_{1}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186410059 :question/worked-solution "Since $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={b}_{1}-5{b}_{2} \\\\  & =-5{b}_{2}+{b}_{1} \\\\  & =-(5{b}_{2})-(-{b}_{1}) \\\\  & =-(5{b}_{2})-\\frac{1}{3}(-3{b}_{1}) \\\\  & =-{c}_{1}-\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].$" "Since $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={b}_{1}-5{b}_{2} \\\\  & =-5{b}_{2}+{b}_{1} \\\\  & =-(5{b}_{2})-(-{b}_{1}) \\\\  & =-(5{b}_{2})-\\frac{1}{3}(-3{b}_{1}) \\\\  & =-{c}_{1}-\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186410060 :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}},$ where ${c}_{1}=\\frac{3}{4}{b}_{3}$, ${c}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{3}{b}_{1}$ and ${c}_{3}=4{b}_{2}?$" "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}},$ where ${c}_{1}=\\frac{3}{4}{b}_{3}$, ${c}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{3}{b}_{1}$ and ${c}_{3}=4{b}_{2}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186410060 :question/worked-solution "Since $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{b}_{1}-8{b}_{2}+3{b}_{3} \\\\  & =3{b}_{3}+2{b}_{1}-8{b}_{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}(\\frac{3}{4}{b}_{3})-2\\cdot 3(-\\frac{1}{3}{b}_{1})-2(4{b}_{2}) \\\\  & =4{c}_{1}-6{c}_{2}-2{c}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$" "Since $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{b}_{1}-8{b}_{2}+3{b}_{3} \\\\  & =3{b}_{3}+2{b}_{1}-8{b}_{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}(\\frac{3}{4}{b}_{3})-2\\cdot 3(-\\frac{1}{3}{b}_{1})-2(4{b}_{2}) \\\\  & =4{c}_{1}-6{c}_{2}-2{c}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186410061 :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}=-2{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=-3{b}_{1}?$" "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}=-2{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=-3{b}_{1}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186410061 :question/worked-solution "Since $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5{b}_{1}-2{b}_{2} \\\\  & =-2{b}_{2}+5{b}_{1} \\\\  & =-2{b}_{2}-\\frac{5}{3}(-3{b}_{1}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}-\\frac{5}{3}{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}].$" "Since $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5{b}_{1}-2{b}_{2} \\\\  & =-2{b}_{2}+5{b}_{1} \\\\  & =-2{b}_{2}-\\frac{5}{3}(-3{b}_{1}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}-\\frac{5}{3}{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186410062 :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}={b}_{1}+{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}={b}_{2}-{b}_{1}?$" "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}={b}_{1}+{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}={b}_{2}-{b}_{1}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186410062 :question/worked-solution "We are given that\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}={b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}={b}_{2}-{b}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. By adding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=2{b}_{2}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}={b}_{1}+(\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2})\\,⟹\\,{b}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =7{b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\  & =7(\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2})+(\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}{c}_{1}-\\frac{7}{2}{c}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2} \\\\  & =4{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$" "We are given that\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}={b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}={b}_{2}-{b}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. By adding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=2{b}_{2}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}={b}_{1}+(\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2})\\,⟹\\,{b}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =7{b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\  & =7(\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2})+(\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}{c}_{1}-\\frac{7}{2}{c}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2} \\\\  & =4{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186410165 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410166 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410167 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410168 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410169 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410170 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410171 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410172 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410173 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410174 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410175 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410176 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410177 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410178 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410179 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410180 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410181 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410182 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410183 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410184 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410185 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410186 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410187 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410188 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410189 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410195 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=1$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}={x}_{2},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 16 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual.\n\nSince we are told that the maximum value of the quadratic form is $20,$ we have\n\n$$\nmax\\{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{max}=20.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=20:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-20I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 4\\end{aligned}]-20[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 16 \\\\ 16 & -16\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-20I)y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get  \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{max}} & =P{u}_{\\text{max}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=1$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}={x}_{2},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 16 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual.\n\nSince we are told that the maximum value of the quadratic form is $20,$ we have\n\n$$\nmax\\{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{max}=20.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=20:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-20I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 4\\end{bmatrix}-20\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 16 \\\\ 16 & -16\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-20I)y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get  \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{max}} & =P{u}_{\\text{max}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186410196 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186410197 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=3$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{3},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual.\n\nSince we are told that the maximum value of the quadratic form is $25,$ we have\n\n$$\nmax\\{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{max}=25.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=25:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-25I & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{aligned}]-25[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -16\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-25I)y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=5,$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{max}} & =P{u}_{\\text{max}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=3$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{3},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual.\n\nSince we are told that the maximum value of the quadratic form is $25,$ we have\n\n$$\nmax\\{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{max}=25.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=25:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-25I & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{bmatrix}-25\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -16\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-25I)y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=5,$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{max}} & =P{u}_{\\text{max}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186410198 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe  maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]-(-4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-4)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$  \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe  maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-(-4)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-4)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$  \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186410240 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410258 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410305 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410320 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410323 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410423 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nGiven the matrices above, write $A{x}^{T}⊙(y+{x}^{T})$ as a matrix product.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$A{x}^{T}⊙(y+{x}^{T})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n\n{{field-2}}\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"7px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n\n{{field-3}}\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">\n\n$0$\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n\n$5$\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n\n$4$\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nGiven the matrices above, write $A{x}^{T}⊙(y+{x}^{T})$ as a matrix product.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$A{x}^{T}⊙(y+{x}^{T})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n\n{{field-2}}\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"7px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n\n{{field-3}}\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">\n\n$0$\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n\n$5$\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n\n$4$\n\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186410423 :question/worked-solution "The Hadamard product of two compatible vectors $u$ and $v$ can be expressed as the matrix product\n\n$$\nv⊙u=\\text{diag}(v)u.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA{x}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny+{x}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that they are of the same size; hence, their Hadamard product is well-defined. We conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{x}^{T}⊙(y+{x}^{T}) & =\\text{diag}(A{x}^{T})(y+{x}^{T}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The Hadamard product of two compatible vectors $u$ and $v$ can be expressed as the matrix product\n\n$$\nv⊙u=\\text{diag}(v)u.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA{x}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny+{x}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that they are of the same size; hence, their Hadamard product is well-defined. We conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{x}^{T}⊙(y+{x}^{T}) & =\\text{diag}(A{x}^{T})(y+{x}^{T}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186410431 :question/problem "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample covariance matrix of the data." "$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample covariance matrix of the data."] [:db/cas 17592186410431 :question/worked-solution "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC=\\frac{1}{n-1}B{B}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nSo, we first need to compute the mean-deviation form of our observation matrix.\n\nThe sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample covariance matrix is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{3-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC=\\frac{1}{n-1}B{B}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nSo, we first need to compute the mean-deviation form of our observation matrix.\n\nThe sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample covariance matrix is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{3-1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186410546 :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]})=$" "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}})=$"] [:db/cas 17592186410546 :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $3$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore,\n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]})=3.$" "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $3$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore,\n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}})=3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186410547 :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]})=$" "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}})=$"] [:db/cas 17592186410547 :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 14\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]})=2.$" "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}})=2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186410548 :question/problem "Determine the dimension of $\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]}.$" "Determine the dimension of $\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186410548 :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 8 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 16\\end{align