egin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{5} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{5} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{4}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410561 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the following matrix: \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Find the inverse of the following matrix: \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186410561 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given matrix is diagonal.\n\nThe inverse of a diagonal matrix can be found as follows: \n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & {a}_{22} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & {a}_{nn}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{a}_{11}} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{{a}_{22}} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & \\frac{1}{{a}_{nn}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIn our case, we obtain\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}={[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Notice that the given matrix is diagonal.\n\nThe inverse of a diagonal matrix can be found as follows: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & {a}_{22} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & {a}_{nn}\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{{a}_{11}} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{{a}_{22}} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & \\frac{1}{{a}_{nn}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we obtain\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}={\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{4}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186410771 :question/problem "The row echelon form matrix $A$ is given below. What are its **non**-pivot columns?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 & 3 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The row echelon form matrix $A$ is given below. What are its **non**-pivot columns?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 & 3 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186410771 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $A$ has $3$ pivots in the $2$nd, $4$th and $5$th columns:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 & 3 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only non-pivot columns are the $1$st and $3$rd columns." "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $A$ has $3$ pivots in the $2$nd, $4$th and $5$th columns:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 & 3 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only non-pivot columns are the $1$st and $3$rd columns."] [:db/cas 17592186410781 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410782 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410784 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410785 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410789 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410791 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410794 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410795 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410797 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410799 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410810 :question/problem "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14} & 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{14} & -3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{14} & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,R=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14} & 3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{14} & -3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{14} & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & 3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186410810 :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14} & 3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14} & \\sqrt{14} & \\sqrt{14} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3} & -3\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14} & 3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}-3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14}{x}_{1}+3\\sqrt{14}{x}_{2}=-3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{3}{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14}{x}_{1}+3\\sqrt{14}(0) & =-3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\sqrt{14}{x}_{1} & =-3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & 3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & \\sqrt{14} & \\sqrt{14} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3} & -3\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & 3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14}{x}_{1}+3\\sqrt{14}{x}_{2}=-3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{3}{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14}{x}_{1}+3\\sqrt{14}(0) & =-3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\sqrt{14}{x}_{1} & =-3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186410811 :question/problem "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} & -1 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,R=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2} & -4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & -1 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & -4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186410811 :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2} & -4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} & 2\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1 & -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2} & -4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}38\\sqrt{2} \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}-4\\sqrt{2}{x}_{2}=38\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-5.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}-4\\sqrt{2}(-5) & =38\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1} & =18\\sqrt{2} \\\\ {x}_{1} & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].$" "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & -4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & 2\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1 & -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & -4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}38\\sqrt{2} \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}-4\\sqrt{2}{x}_{2}=38\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-5.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}-4\\sqrt{2}(-5) & =38\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1} & =18\\sqrt{2} \\\\ {x}_{1} & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186410831 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410832 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410833 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410834 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410835 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410836 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410838 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410840 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410843 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410844 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410846 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410847 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410848 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410849 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410850 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186410855 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}16 & -10 \\\\ 17 & -10\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A$ has the complex eigenvalue $\\lambda =3-\\text{i},$ and that $A=PC{P}^{-1}$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}16 & -10 \\\\ 17 & -10\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A$ has the complex eigenvalue $\\lambda =3-\\text{i},$ and that $A=PC{P}^{-1}$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186410855 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nSince we are given the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3-\\text{i},$ we can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=3-\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & -10 \\\\ 17 & -10\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3-\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}13+\\text{i} & -10 \\\\ 17 & -13+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(13+\\text{i}){x}_{1}-10{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 17{x}_{1}+(-13+\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{10}(13+\\text{i}){x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{10}(13+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=10$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 13+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 13 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{10}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{{13}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{10}{17}.\n$$" "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nSince we are given the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3-\\text{i},$ we can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=3-\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 & -10 \\\\ 17 & -10\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3-\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3-\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}13+\\text{i} & -10 \\\\ 17 & -13+\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(13+\\text{i}){x}_{1}-10{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 17{x}_{1}+(-13+\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{10}(13+\\text{i}){x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{10}(13+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=10$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 13+\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 13 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{10}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{{13}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{10}{17}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186410856 :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1+3\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1+3\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186410856 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=-1-3\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i} \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=-1-3\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―