bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186413789 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ngiven that the general solution to our differential equation is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{2t}+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{-t}.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |  |" "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ngiven that the general solution to our differential equation is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{2t}+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{-t}.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186413789 :question/worked-solution "To find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=2 \\\\ {c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n3{c}_{1}=9\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n3+{c}_{2}=2\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+(-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=2 \\\\ {c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n3{c}_{1}=9\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n3+{c}_{2}=2\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186413790 :question/problem "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned},\\,x(1)=[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{2} \\\\ 3{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$*" "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned},\\,x(1)=\\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{2} \\\\ 3{e}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186413790 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=5\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=1.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=1$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(1) & =[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{2} \\\\ 3{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]e & =[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{2} \\\\ 3{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{5}{c}_{1}+3e{c}_{2} \\\\ {e}^{5}{c}_{1}+e{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{2} \\\\ 3{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{5}{c}_{1}+3e{c}_{2}=5{e}^{2} \\\\ {e}^{5}{c}_{1}+e{c}_{2}=3{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4e{c}_{2}=8{e}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=2e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${c}_{2}=2e$ into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5}{c}_{1}+e(2e)=3{e}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}={e}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{-3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+2e[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=5\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=1.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=1$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{2} \\\\ 3{e}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e & =\\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{2} \\\\ 3{e}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{5}{c}_{1}+3e{c}_{2} \\\\ {e}^{5}{c}_{1}+e{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{2} \\\\ 3{e}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{5}{c}_{1}+3e{c}_{2}=5{e}^{2} \\\\ {e}^{5}{c}_{1}+e{c}_{2}=3{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4e{c}_{2}=8{e}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=2e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${c}_{2}=2e$ into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5}{c}_{1}+e(2e)=3{e}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}={e}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{-3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+2e\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186413791 :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix with eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2$ corresponding to the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ respectively, where\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nGiven that the position of a particle at time $t$ seconds is given by the vector $x(t),$ and that $x(t)$ is the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nfind the position of the particle at $t=3$ seconds." "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix with eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2$ corresponding to the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ respectively, where\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the position of a particle at time $t$ seconds is given by the vector $x(t),$ and that $x(t)$ is the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nfind the position of the particle at $t=3$ seconds."] [:db/cas 17592186413791 :question/worked-solution "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=3$ into the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}=2 \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get\n\n$$\n-{c}_{2}=6\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=-6.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+(-6)=4\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=10.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =10\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+(-6)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $x(3)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(3) & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3\\cdot 3}+[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]{e}^{9}+[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-6} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10{e}^{9}+12{e}^{-6} \\\\ 10{e}^{9}-6{e}^{-6}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=3$ into the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}=2 \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get\n\n$$\n-{c}_{2}=6\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=-6.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+(-6)=4\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=10.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =10\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+(-6)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $x(3)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(3) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3\\cdot 3}+\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{9}+\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-6} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-10{e}^{9}+12{e}^{-6} \\\\ 10{e}^{9}-6{e}^{-6}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186413832 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =1.$*" "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =1.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186413832 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =1$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2-1 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 4-1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3{w}_{1}+9{w}_{2}=3 \\\\ -1{w}_{1}+3{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=3{w}_{2}-1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get ${w}_{1}=-1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=3,b=-1,$ and $a+b=2.$" "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =1$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2-1 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 4-1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3{w}_{1}+9{w}_{2}=3 \\\\ -1{w}_{1}+3{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=3{w}_{2}-1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get ${w}_{1}=-1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=3,b=-1,$ and $a+b=2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186413947 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186413948 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186413949 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186413950 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186413951 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186413953 :question/worked-solution "The third equation states that $z=3.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get  \n\n$$\ny+(3)=2\\,⟹\\,y=2-3=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we substitute $z=3$ and $y=-1$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx-(-1)-(3)=4\\,⟹\\,x=4-1+3=6.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=6$, $y=-1$, and $z=3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n$[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" "The third equation states that $z=3.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get  \n\n$$\ny+(3)=2\\,⟹\\,y=2-3=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we substitute $z=3$ and $y=-1$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx-(-1)-(3)=4\\,⟹\\,x=4-1+3=6.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=6$, $y=-1$, and $z=3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186413955 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-92 & 62 \\\\ -186 & 125\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-92 & 62 \\\\ -186 & 125\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186413962 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}72 & -52 \\\\ -166 & 75\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}72 & -52 \\\\ -166 & 75\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186413963 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2e & {e}^{3}-4e \\\\ e & {e}^{3}-2e\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2e & {e}^{3}-4e \\\\ e & {e}^{3}-2e\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186413964 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}62 & 92 \\\\ -125 & 186\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}62 & 92 \\\\ -125 & 186\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186413969 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3}+2e & -4e \\\\ e & {e}^{3}-2e\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3}+2e & -4e \\\\ e & {e}^{3}-2e\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186413970 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}32 & 24 \\\\ -18 & 180\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}32 & 24 \\\\ -18 & 180\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186413972 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3e & -4e \\\\ e & -e\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3e & -4e \\\\ e & -e\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186413973 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}31 & 46 \\\\ -67 & 98\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}31 & 46 \\\\ -67 & 98\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186413976 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3} & -4{e}^{3} \\\\ {e}^{3} & -{e}^{3}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{3} & -4{e}^{3} \\\\ {e}^{3} & -{e}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186413978 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3{e}^{3} & 2{e}^{3} \\\\ 2{e}^{3} & -{e}^{3}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-3{e}^{3} & 2{e}^{3} \\\\ 2{e}^{3} & -{e}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186414003 :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=[\\begin{aligned}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $f(x)={e}^{x},$ $n=3,$ and $\\lambda =1.$\n\nSince $n=3,$ we need to compute the first $3-1=2$ derivative of $f:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{3}(1)) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(1) & {f}^{′}(1) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{2!} \\\\ 0 & f(1) & {f}^{′}(1) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f(1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}e & e & \\frac{e}{2} \\\\ 0 & e & e \\\\ 0 & 0 & e\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $f(x)={e}^{x},$ $n=3,$ and $\\lambda =1.$\n\nSince $n=3,$ we need to compute the first $3-1=2$ derivative of $f:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{3}(1)) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(1) & {f}^{′}(1) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{2!} \\\\ 0 & f(1) & {f}^{′}(1) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f(1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}e & e & \\frac{e}{2} \\\\ 0 & e & e \\\\ 0 & 0 & e\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186414004 :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $X=PD{P}^{-1},$ find ${X}^{5}.$" "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $X=PD{P}^{-1},$ find ${X}^{5}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186414004 :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SA{S}^{-1})=S\\cdot f(A)\\cdot {S}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x)={x}^{5}$ and the given matrix $X,$ we have\n\n$$\n{X}^{5}=(PD{P}^{-1})^{5}=P\\cdot ({D}^{5})\\cdot {P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $D$ is a diagonal matrix, we get\n\n$$\n{D}^{5}=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{5}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 32\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\frac{2}{2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{5} & =P\\cdot ({D}^{5})\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 32\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -96 & 64\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-92 & 62 \\\\ -186 & 125\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SA{S}^{-1})=S\\cdot f(A)\\cdot {S}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x)={x}^{5}$ and the given matrix $X,$ we have\n\n$$\n{X}^{5}=(PD{P}^{-1})^{5}=P\\cdot ({D}^{5})\\cdot {P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $D$ is a diagonal matrix, we get\n\n$$\n{D}^{5}=\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{5}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 32\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\frac{2}{2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{5} & =P\\cdot ({D}^{5})\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 32\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -96 & 64\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-92 & 62 \\\\ -186 & 125\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186414005 :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=[\\begin{aligned}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{({J}_{1}(3)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{3}(0))} & ={e}^{([\\begin{aligned}{J}_{1}(3) & 0 \\\\ 0 & {J}_{3}(0)\\end{aligned}])} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{({J}_{1}(3))} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{({J}_{3}(0))}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{({J}_{1}(3))}\\,⊕\\,{e}^{({J}_{3}(0))}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the function of each Jordan block separately.\n\n- For ${J}_{1}(3),$ we have $n=1$ and $\\lambda =3.$ since $n=1,$ we don't need any derivatives. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{1}(3)) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(3)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${J}_{3}(0),$ we have $n=3$ and $\\lambda =2.$ \\since $n=3,$ we need to compute the first and second derivatives of $f$ only:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{3}(0)) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(0) & {f}^{′}(0) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(0)}{2} \\\\ 0 & f(0) & {f}^{′}(0) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f(0)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{0} & {e}^{0} & \\frac{{e}^{0}}{2} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{0} & {e}^{0} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {e}^{0}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{({J}_{1}(3)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{3}(0))} & ={e}^{({J}_{1}(3))}\\,⊕\\,{e}^{({J}_{3}(0))} \\\\  & 