7592186414186 :question/worked-solution "First, we set up the augmented matrix for the system:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 & 12 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 13\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 & 12 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 13\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 13\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=4 \\\\ 0x+0y=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the final equation $0x+0y=-3$ has no solution. It simplifies to $0=-3,$ which is a false statement.\n\nSo, there is no pair $x,y$ that satisfies both equations in the system. Consequently, the overall system has no solution." "First, we set up the augmented matrix for the system:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 & 12 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 & 12 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=4 \\\\ 0x+0y=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the final equation $0x+0y=-3$ has no solution. It simplifies to $0=-3,$ which is a false statement.\n\nSo, there is no pair $x,y$ that satisfies both equations in the system. Consequently, the overall system has no solution."] [:db/cas 17592186414494 :question/problem "Determine the number of pivot columns of the matrix $P,$ given by\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 2 & 7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Determine the number of pivot columns of the matrix $P,$ given by\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 2 & 7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186414494 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $P$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 2 & 7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 2 & 7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 2 & 7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $P$ has $4$ pivots, and therefore the number of pivot columns is $4.$" "First, we need to convert $P$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 2 & 7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 2 & 7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 2 & 7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $P$ has $4$ pivots, and therefore the number of pivot columns is $4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186414495 :question/problem "The row echelon form matrix $A$ is given below. What are the pivot columns of $A?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The row echelon form matrix $A$ is given below. What are the pivot columns of $A?$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186414495 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $A$ has $3$ pivots in the $1$st, $3$rd, and $5$th columns:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $A$ has $3$ pivots in the $1$st, $3$rd, and $5$th columns:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186414496 :question/problem "The row echelon form matrix $M$ is given below. What are the pivot columns of $M?$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The row echelon form matrix $M$ is given below. What are the pivot columns of $M?$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186414496 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $M$ has $3$ pivots in the $2$nd, $3$rd, and $5$th columns:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $M$ has $3$ pivots in the $2$nd, $3$rd, and $5$th columns:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186414497 :question/problem "The row echelon form matrix $B$ is given below. What are its **non**-pivot columns?\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The row echelon form matrix $B$ is given below. What are its **non**-pivot columns?\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186414497 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $B$ has $3$ pivots in the $1$st, $3$rd and $4$th columns:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only non-pivot column is the $2$nd column." "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $B$ has $3$ pivots in the $1$st, $3$rd and $4$th columns:\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only non-pivot column is the $2$nd column."] [:db/cas 17592186414500 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186414501 :answer/value "{e}^{6t}[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" "{e}^{6t}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186414503 :answer/value "{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" "{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186414504 :answer/value "{e}^{6t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}])" "{e}^{6t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix})"] [:db/cas 17592186414517 :answer/value "{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" "{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186414540 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$\\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186414540 :question/worked-solution "We are told that the general solution to the system is \n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-0} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0\\cdot {e}^{-0} \\\\ {e}^{-0}\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{2(0)}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+1 \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+1=2 \\\\ {c}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get ${c}_{1}=1.$ From the second equation, we have ${c}_{2}=1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\nx(t)=1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "We are told that the general solution to the system is \n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-0} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0\\cdot {e}^{-0} \\\\ {e}^{-0}\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{2(0)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1}+1 \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+1=2 \\\\ {c}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get ${c}_{1}=1.$ From the second equation, we have ${c}_{2}=1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\nx(t)=1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186414541 :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}-3{t}^{2}+4t-4 \\\\ -{t}^{2}-2t+1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t+2 \\\\ t+1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n| $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+\\,\\,$ |  | $1$ |  | $\\,\\,{t}^{2}+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,t+\\,\\,$ |  | $1$ |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  | $2$ |  |  | {{field-3}} |  |  |  |  |  |" "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}-3{t}^{2}+4t-4 \\\\ -{t}^{2}-2t+1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t+2 \\\\ t+1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n| $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+\\,\\,$ |  | $1$ |  | $\\,\\,{t}^{2}+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,t+\\,\\,$ |  | $1$ |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  | $2$ |  |  | {{field-3}} |  |  |  |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186414541 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3{t}^{2}+4t-4 \\\\ -{t}^{2}-2t+1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}{t}^{2}+{b}_{1}t+{c}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}{t}^{2}+{b}_{2}t+{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]{t}^{2}+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]{t}^{2}+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}-3{t}^{2}+4t-4 \\\\ -{t}^{2}-2t+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2({a}_{1}{t}^{2}+{b}_{1}t+{c}_{1})-({a}_{2}{t}^{2}+{b}_{2}t+{c}_{2}) \\\\ {a}_{1}{t}^{2}+{b}_{1}t+{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3{t}^{2}+4t-4 \\\\ -{t}^{2}-2t+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0{t}^{2}+2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 0{t}^{2}+2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}(2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}-3){t}^{2}+(2{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}+4)t+(2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}-4) \\\\ ({a}_{1}-1){t}^{2}+({b}_{1}-2)t+({c}_{1}+1)\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}-3=0 \\\\ 2{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}+4=2{a}_{1} \\\\ 2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}-4={b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1}-1=0 \\\\ {b}_{1}-2=2{a}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+1={b}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the fourth equation and then substituting the corresponding values ​​into the first and fifth equations to solve for ${a}_{2}$ and ${b}_{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=1,\\,{a}_{2}=-1,\\,{b}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe substitute these into the second equation to solve for ${b}_{2},$ and then into the sixth and third equations to solve for ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ We finally get\n\n$$\n{b}_{2}=2,\\,{c}_{1}=-1,\\,{c}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+1 \\\\ -\\,{t}^{2}+2t-2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{t}^{2}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3{t}^{2}+4t-4 \\\\ -{t}^{2}-2t+1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}{t}^{2}+{b}_{1}t+{c}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}{t}^{2}+{b}_{2}t+{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2{a}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}2{a}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix})+\\begin{bmatrix}-3{t}^{2}+4t-4 \\\\ -{t}^{2}-2t+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2({a}_{1}{t}^{2}+{b}_{1}t+{c}_{1})-({a}_{2}{t}^{2}+{b}_{2}t+{c}_{2}) \\\\ {a}_{1}{t}^{2}+{b}_{1}t+{c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3{t}^{2}+4t-4 \\\\ -{t}^{2}-2t+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0{t}^{2}+2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 0{t}^{2}+2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}(2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}-3){t}^{2}+(2{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}+4)t+(2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}-4) \\\\ ({a}_{1}-1){t}^{2}+({b}_{1}-2)t+({c}_{1}+1)\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}-3=0 \\\\ 2{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}+4=2{a}_{1} \\\\ 2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}-4={b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1}-1=0 \\\\ {b}_{1}-2=2{a}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+1={b}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the fourth equation and then substituting the corresponding values ​​into the first and fifth equations to solve for ${a}_{2}$ and ${b}_{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=1,\\,{a}_{2}=-1,\\,{b}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe substitute these into the second equation to solve for ${b}_{2},$ and then into the sixth and third equations to solve for ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ We finally get\n\n$$\n{b}_{2}=2,\\,{c}_{1}=-1,\\,{c}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+1 \\\\ -\\,{t}^{2}+2t-2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186414542 :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+{e}^{6t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nA complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as \n$x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),$\nwhere ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$" "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+{e}^{6t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nA complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as \n$x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),$\nwhere ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$"] [:db/cas 17592186414542 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere (\\mathbf{x}_c(t)) is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n{e}^{6t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is exponential. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution has the same exponential factor, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{6t}[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined." "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere (\\mathbf{x}_c(t)) is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n{e}^{6t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is exponential. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution has the same exponential factor, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{6t}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined."] [:db/cas 17592186414543 :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=(-1)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+2\\cdot {t}^{2}+(-1)\\cdot t+1 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+(-2)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+1\\cdot {t}^{2}+4\\cdot t+(-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{t}^{2}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}].$" "First, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=(-1)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+2\\cdot {t}^{2}+(-1)\\cdot t+1 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+(-2)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+1\\cdot {t}^{2}+4\\cdot t+(-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186414544 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}]\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$\\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{bmatrix}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186414544 :question/worked-solution "We are told that the general solution to the system is \n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-2(0)}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+1 \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+1=0 \\\\ {c}_{2}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get ${c}_{1}=-1.$ From the second equation, we have ${c}_{2}=3.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\nx(t)=-1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}]+3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "We are told that the general solution to the system is \n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{-2(0)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1}+1 \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+1=0 \\\\ {c}_{2}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get ${c}_{1}=-1.$ From the second equation, we have ${c}_{2}=3.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\nx(t)=-1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{bmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186414546 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8-3y-2z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}8-3y-2z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186414547 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4-3y+2z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}4-3y+2z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186414548 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+z[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+z\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186414550 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+z[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+z\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186414551 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4+3y-2z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}-4+3y-2z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186414552 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+z[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+z\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186414553 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+z[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+z\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186414554 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5+2y+3z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}5+2y+3z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186414557 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 7 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix."] [:db/cas 17592186414557 :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 7 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+3z=1 \\\\ 0x+y+z=1 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just two equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+3z=1 \\\\ y+z=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ To do this, we apply the back substitution. \n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+z=1\\,⇒\\,y=1-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2(1-z)+3z=1\\,⇒\\,x=-1-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1-z \\\\ y=1-z \\\\ z=z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1-z \\\\ 1-z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+z[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+3z=1 \\\\ 0x+y+z=1 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just two equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+3z=1 \\\\ y+z=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ To do this, we apply the back substitution. \n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+z=1\\,⇒\\,y=1-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2(1-z)+3z=1\\,⇒\\,x=-1-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1-z \\\\ y=1-z \\\\ z=z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1-z \\\\ 1-z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+z\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186414662 :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vectors $v=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" "Find the acute angle between the vectors $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $w=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186414663 :question/problem "Rounded to two decimal places, what is the acute angle between the vectors $v=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]?$" "Rounded to two decimal places, what is the acute angle between the vectors $v=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $w=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186414668 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186414669 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186414670 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186414671 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186414672 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186414750 :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{7} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{14}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|a|+|b|+|c|?$" "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{7} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{14}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|a|+|b|+|c|?$"] [:db/cas 17592186414750 :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 3}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis.\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{7} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{14}\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{7} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{14}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthonormal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10},$ $c=-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|\\,0\\,|+|\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10}|+|-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}|=\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\n$$" "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 3}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis.\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{7} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{14}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{7} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{14}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is an orthonormal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10},$ $c=-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|\\,0\\,|+|\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10}|+|-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}|=\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186414751 :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186414751 :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote \n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 0+(-3)\\cdot (-1)}{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, we have $a=-\\frac{1}{2},$ $b=2,$ and \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}=-4.\n$$" "First, let's denote \n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 0+(-3)\\cdot (-1)}{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, we have $a=-\\frac{1}{2},$ $b=2,$ and \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}=-4.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186414752 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186414752 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-3)\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 2}{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=1,$ $b=0,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.\n$$" "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-3)\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 2}{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=1,$ $b=0,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186414753 :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]},\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" "$$\nV=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}},\\,B={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186414753 :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote \n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-2)}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{17}{6} \\\\ \\frac{11}{6} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{17}{6} \\\\ \\frac{11}{6} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}]}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, we have $a=\\frac{17}{6},$ $b=\\frac{11}{6},$ and \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{(\\frac{17}{6})}{(\\frac{11}{6})}=\\frac{17}{11}.\n$$" "First, let's denote \n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-2)}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{17}{6} \\\\ \\frac{11}{6} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{17}{6} \\\\ \\frac{11}{6} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3}\\end{bmatrix}}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, we have $a=\\frac{17}{6},$ $b=\\frac{11}{6},$ and \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{(\\frac{17}{6})}{(\\frac{11}{6})}=\\frac{17}{11}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186415139 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ {x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $12a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ {x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $12a+\\frac{b}{c}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186415139 :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 6 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 6 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 54\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 30\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -18 & -10 \\\\ -18 & 54 & 30\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & -18 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{3}+3{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{3}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n12a+\\frac{b}{c}=12\\cdot (-\\frac{5}{3})+\\frac{3}{1}=-17.\n$$" "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 6 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 6 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}6 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 54\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 30\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -18 & -10 \\\\ -18 & 54 & 30\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -18 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{3}+3{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{3}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n12a+\\frac{b}{c}=12\\cdot (-\\frac{5}{3})+\\frac{3}{1}=-17.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186415140 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+3b+c?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+3b+c?$"] [:db/cas 17592186415140 :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6 & -6 & -4 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -6 & -4 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 4 & 4 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -6 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -6 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+6{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{3}=-2,\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+(-2)=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-1+{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+3b+c=0+3\\cdot (-2)+1=-5.\n$$" "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}6 & -6 & -4 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -6 & -4 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 4 & 4 & -4\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -6 & -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -6 & -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+6{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{3}=-2,\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+(-2)=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-1+{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+3b+c=0+3\\cdot (-2)+1=-5.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186415141 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $6a+\\frac{4b}{c}?$" "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $6a+\\frac{4b}{c}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186415141 :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}24 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}24 & -12 & 4 \\\\ -12 & 6 & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}24 & -12 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n6a+\\frac{4b}{c}=6\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}}{1}=3.\n$$" "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}24 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}24 & -12 & 4 \\\\ -12 & 6 & -2\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}24 & -12 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n6a+\\frac{4b}{c}=6\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}}{1}=3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186415142 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $14a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $14a+\\frac{b}{c}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186415142 :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}14 & -28 \\\\ -28 & 56\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & -28 & 9 \\\\ -28 & 56 & -18\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}14 & -28 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{9}{14}+2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{14}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{14} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n14a+\\frac{b}{c}=14\\cdot \\frac{9}{14}+\\frac{2}{1}=11.\n$$" "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}14 & -28 \\\\ -28 & 56\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -18\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -28 & 9 \\\\ -28 & 56 & -18\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -28 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{9}{14}+2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{9}{14}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{9}{14} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n14a+\\frac{b}{c}=14\\cdot \\frac{9}{14}+\\frac{2}{1}=11.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186415143 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $2a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $2a+\\frac{b}{c}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186415143 :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 54\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -18 & -3 \\\\ -18 & 54 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & -18 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}+3{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\n Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$2a+\\frac{b}{c}=2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{3}{1}=2.$" "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}6 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 54\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -18 & -3 \\\\ -18 & 54 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -18 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}+3{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\n Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$2a+\\frac{b}{c}=2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{3}{1}=2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186415156 :question/problem "$$\n{u}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n- **II.** ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthogonal\n- **III.** ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthonormal\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "$$\n{u}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\,{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n- **II.** ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthogonal\n- **III.** ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthonormal\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186415156 :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=12-12=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $Q({u}_{1})⊥Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that, for example, $Q({u}_{1})$ is not a unit vector since\n\n$$\n‖Q({u}_{1})‖=‖{u}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-6)^{2}}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=12-12=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $Q({u}_{1})⊥Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that, for example, $Q({u}_{1})$ is not a unit vector since\n\n$$\n‖Q({u}_{1})‖=‖{u}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-6)^{2}}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186415475 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given determinants in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ is a triangular matrix, its determinant is given by the product of the elements of the main diagonal:\n\n$$\n|A|=0\\cdot 0\\cdot 0\\cdot 0=0\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ has proportional rows: \n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{2} & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 2 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1 & \\sqrt{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nAs a result, $|B|=0.$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has a zero column: \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|C|=0.$\n\nTherefore, $|A|,$ $|B|,$ and $|C|$ are equal to $0.$" "Let's examine each of the given determinants in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ is a triangular matrix, its determinant is given by the product of the elements of the main diagonal:\n\n$$\n|A|=0\\cdot 0\\cdot 0\\cdot 0=0\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ has proportional rows: \n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\sqrt{2} & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 2 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1 & \\sqrt{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \nAs a result, $|B|=0.$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has a zero column: \n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|C|=0.$\n\nTherefore, $|A|,$ $|B|,$ and $|C|$ are equal to $0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186415476 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2.05 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & \\sqrt{7} & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix given above. Identify **all** of the reasons why one can conclude that $|A|=0?$\n- **I.** $A$ has proportional columns\n- **II.** $A$ has identical rows\n- **III.** $A$ has a zero row\n\nSelect all that apply." "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2.05 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & \\sqrt{7} & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix given above. Identify **all** of the reasons why one can conclude that $|A|=0?$\n- **I.** $A$ has proportional columns\n- **II.** $A$ has identical rows\n- **III.** $A$ has a zero row\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186415476 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- $A$ has proportional columns: \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2.05 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & \\sqrt{7} & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $A$ does *not* have identical rows.\n\n- $A$ has a zero row: \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2.05 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & \\sqrt{7} & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- $A$ has proportional columns: \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2.05 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & \\sqrt{7} & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $A$ does *not* have identical rows.\n\n- $A$ has a zero row: \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2.05 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & \\sqrt{7} & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186415477 :question/problem "Find $5det(A)+7det(B)$, where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 3\\sqrt{3} & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{2} & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 1 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find $5det(A)+7det(B)$, where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 3\\sqrt{3} & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{2} & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 1 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186415477 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ has proportional rows, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 3\\sqrt{3} & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,det(A)=0.\n$$\n\n- Since $B$ has a zero column, we obtain\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{2} & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 1 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,det(B)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5det(A)+7det(B)=5\\cdot 0+7\\cdot 0=0.$" "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ has proportional rows, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 3\\sqrt{3} & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,det(A)=0.\n$$\n\n- Since $B$ has a zero column, we obtain\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{2} & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 1 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,det(B)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5det(A)+7det(B)=5\\cdot 0+7\\cdot 0=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186415478 :question/problem "Find $3det(A)-det(B)$, where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0.57 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find $3det(A)-det(B)$, where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0.57 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186415478 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ is a triangular matrix, we have\n\n$$\ndet(A)=2\\cdot 1\\cdot 3=6.\n$$\n\n- Since $B$ has proportional columns, we obtain\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0.57 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,det(B)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $3det(A)-det(B)=3\\cdot 6-0=18.$" "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ is a triangular matrix, we have\n\n$$\ndet(A)=2\\cdot 1\\cdot 3=6.\n$$\n\n- Since $B$ has proportional columns, we obtain\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0.57 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,det(B)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $3det(A)-det(B)=3\\cdot 6-0=18.$"] [:db/cas 17592186415479 :question/worked-solution "Let's consider our statements in turn.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. For example, consider the matrix \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nExpending across the $1$st column, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(3-2)+(1-2) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbut $A$ has neither a zero column nor two proportional rows.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we can make use of the following fact:\n\n$|A|=0$ *if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "Let's consider our statements in turn.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. For example, consider the matrix \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nExpending across the $1$st column, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(3-2)+(1-2) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbut $A$ has neither a zero column nor two proportional rows.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we can make use of the following fact:\n\n$|A|=0$ *if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186415504 :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{31}\\cdot {r}_{11}?$" "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{31}\\cdot {r}_{11}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186415504 :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{31}\\cdot {r}_{11}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}=-2.$" "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{31}\\cdot {r}_{11}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}=-2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186415505 :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{22}?$" "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{22}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186415505 :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{9} & \\frac{4}{9} & \\frac{4}{9} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{9} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{9} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{9} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{22}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}=1.$" "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{9} & \\frac{4}{9} & \\frac{4}{9} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{9} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{9} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{9} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{22}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}=1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186415506 :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. You're given that the system of equations given below connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{2}+{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{3}-{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{11}+{r}_{12}+{r}_{33}?$" "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. You're given that the system of equations given below connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{2}+{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{3}-{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{11}+{r}_{12}+{r}_{33}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186415506 :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{2}+{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{3}-{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}=3{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}=-2{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3}=4{q}_{1}-8{q}_{2}+4{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, this system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{11}+{r}_{12}+{r}_{33}=3+(-2)+4=5.\n$$" "Let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{2}+{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{3}-{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}=3{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}=-2{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3}=4{q}_{1}-8{q}_{2}+4{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, this system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{11}+{r}_{12}+{r}_{33}=3+(-2)+4=5.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186415574 :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]?$" "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186415574 :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -4) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =4,\\,1,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{4}=2 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{1}=1 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{3} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{3}}=\\sqrt{0}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -4) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =4,\\,1,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{4}=2 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{1}=1 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{3} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{3}}=\\sqrt{0}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186415575 :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]?$" "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186415575 :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}8-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (8-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =8,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{8} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}8-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (8-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =8,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{8} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186415576 :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]?$" "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186415576 :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}8-\\lambda  & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}8-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )((8-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +36) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,4,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{4}=2 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{3} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{3}}=\\sqrt{0}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}8-\\lambda  & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}8-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )((8-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +36) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,4,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{4}=2 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{3} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{3}}=\\sqrt{0}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186415577 :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]?$" "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186415577 :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|-4|\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )[(5-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-4)]-16(4-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +16)-16(4-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (4-\\lambda )(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,4,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{4}=2 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{3} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{3}}=\\sqrt{0}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}4-\\lambda  & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}5-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix}-4\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )[(5-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-4)]-16(4-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +16)-16(4-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (4-\\lambda )(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,4,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{4}=2 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{3} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{3}}=\\sqrt{0}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186415578 :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]?$" "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186415578 :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$  \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-2\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =2,\\,1,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{1}=1 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{3} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{3}}=\\sqrt{0}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$  \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-2\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =2,\\,1,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{1}=1 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{3} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{3}}=\\sqrt{0}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186415690 :question/worked-solution "We wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0-1(1-9)+2(1-6) \\\\  & =+8-10 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -8 & -5 \\\\ -1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -6 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 8 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 8 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -8 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -8 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 9 \\\\ -\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=-\\frac{5}{2},y=9,z=-\\frac{9}{2}.$" "We wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-1(1-9)+2(1-6) \\\\  & =+8-10 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -8 & -5 \\\\ -1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -6 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 8 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 8 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -8 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -8 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 9 \\\\ -\\frac{9}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=-\\frac{5}{2},y=9,z=-\\frac{9}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186416323 :question/problem "Find the distance between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find the distance between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416323 :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{16}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3-3)^{2}+(6-1)^{2}+(4-(-1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{50} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{16}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\hat{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\hat{x}) & =‖x-\\hat{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\hat{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\hat{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\hat{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3-3)^{2}+(6-1)^{2}+(4-(-1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{50} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416324 :question/problem "Find the distance between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find the distance between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416324 :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{90}[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}+({x}_{4}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1-2)^{2}+(2-2)^{2}+(-3-(-1))^{2}+(-3-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+0+4+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{90}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\hat{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\hat{x}) & =‖x-\\hat{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\hat{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\hat{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\hat{x}}_{3})^{2}+({x}_{4}-{\\hat{x}}_{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1-2)^{2}+(2-2)^{2}+(-3-(-1))^{2}+(-3-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+0+4+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416326 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416330 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416331 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416332 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416335 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416337 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416340 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416341 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 5 \\\\ \\frac{9}{5}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 5 \\\\ \\frac{9}{5}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416343 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416344 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416356 :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416356 :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 18\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-1)}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{8}\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 18\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-1)}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{8}\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."] [:db/cas 17592186416357 :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416357 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis for the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is the basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nNow, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we need to find a basis for the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is the basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nNow, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416358 :question/problem "Find the distance between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find the distance between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"]]
